# early-exit-readers.manifest — the CHECKED boundary for your-org/nexus-code#682.
#
# Generated by:  bash monitor/watcher/early-exit-readers.sh
# Enforced by:   bash monitor/watcher/test-early-exit-reader-manifest.sh
#
# One row per (file, kind, scope) with a COUNT. Keyed on counts, not line
# numbers, so ordinary edits are silent and only a NEW or REMOVED early-exit
# reader moves a number. The declared axis lives in the classifier's header —
# read it before regenerating this file.
#
# REGENERATING THIS FILE IS NOT A FIX. If the suite goes red, a reader that can
# close a pipe early was added or removed. Decide which it is, and say so in the
# commit message:
#   * a NEW site — is the writer's EPIPE reachable under `pipefail`? If the
#     pipeline's status is consumed (`if`, `&&`, `set -e`, a captured `$?`),
#     that is the #622 defect with a different reader. Fix it, do not record it.
#   * a REMOVED site — good; regenerate.
#   * a scope flip — a file moved between prod and test.
#
# `sed-q` is legitimately ABSENT: of every `| sed …` site on the axis, none
# carries a quit command, so none can close its pipe early. That zero is a
# measured result, and it is why "convert every `| sed -n`" would have been
# 54 changes with no defect behind them. `grep -q` is excluded on purpose —
# test-sigpipe-assertion-lint.sh owns it, and double-covering would
# double-report every site.
#
# file<TAB>kind<TAB>scope<TAB>count
#
# SCOPE, DECIDED (your-org/nexus-code#1130). An EXECUTED COMMAND STRING IS ITS
# OWN PIPEFAIL SCOPE — see the classifier header, axis item 5. Three effects on
# this file, each measured against the 114-row manifest at `ca6205c`:
#
#   +1  monitor/watcher/test-service-health-selfmatch.sh  head  test  1
#       The `#1130` worked example, and a TRUE positive that was absent. That
#       file sets only `set -u` and has zero sourcers, so it is off the FILE
#       axis; its `:156` `"set -o pipefail; pgrep -f $M | head -1"` enables the
#       option in the string the runner evaluates, and the `| head -1` in that
#       same string really does run under it. Its `:152` (`"false | head -1"`)
#       is correctly still absent — that string carries no pipefail.
#   -1  monitor/watcher/test-fm-lib.sh        (2 -> 1)
#   -1  monitor/watcher/test-remote-enroll.sh (3 -> 2)
#       Both are PROSE: `echo "… old sed|head -1 …"` in a section banner. The
#       pipe is inside a string that enables nothing, so it is not a site.
#
# Nothing else moved. The classifier now decides scope from whether the PIPE is
# quoted rather than from whether the file is on the axis, and that reading was
# cross-checked against the two alternatives before it shipped: masking per line
# alone loses `_github.sh:374` (a `| head -n1` after a six-line single-quoted
# `jq` program) and carrying the mask alone loses eight, including
# `monitor/ng:6354`. "Quoted" is therefore believed only when both readings
# agree.
